7633224/b905e82e_c1da23c6.text:
Wow, amazing finding. Thank you for raising this! I looked into this a bit - let me share my understanding:
- For some Windows APIs, GUIDs are defined as follows ([ref1](https://godoc.corp.google.com/pkg/google3/third_party/golang/ole/ole?content_ref=guid+struct+data1+uint32+data2+uint16+data3+uint16+data4+8+byte+guid+is+windows+api+specific+guid+type+this+exists+to+match+windows+guid+type+for+direct+passing+for+com#GUID), [ref2](https://devblogs.microsoft.com/oldnewthing/20220928-00/?p=107221)):
```
struct GUID {
  uint32_t Data1;
  uint16_t Data2;
  uint16_t Data3;
  uint8_t  Data4[8];
}
```
  - On little endian machines, this means that the bytes of the first three parts are stored in reverse order. Specifically, if the Uuid is `"00112233-4455-6677-8899-AABBCCDDEEFF"`, the memory layout in the following scenarios is:
    - Logical and as per RFC4122: `00 11 22 33 44 55 66 77 88 99 AA BB CC DD EE FF`
    - Microsoft GUID on
      - Big endian machines: `00 11 22 33 44 55 66 77 88 99 AA BB CC DD EE FF`
      - Little endian machines `33 22 11 00 55 44 77 66 88 99 AA BB CC DD EE FF`
    - My current implementation on
      - Big endian machines: `00 11 22 33 44 55 66 77 88 99 AA BB CC DD EE FF`
      - Little endian machines: `FF EE DD CC BB AA 99 88 77 66 55 44 33 22 11 00`
- My implementation is problematic in the sense that on little endian machines, it matches neither of the two standards. This can be a problem if somebody interpretes or stores the result of `AsInteger()`

